      "content": "Yes, exactly! That follows the instruction precisely and ensures you don't lose unnecessary marks.\n\n**Model Answer for Exam:**\n> (a) D\n> (b) C\n> (c) E\n> (d) A\n\n---\n### 📖 Concept & Thought Process (How to Understand It):\n* **The Concept / Rule:** Character matching questions in setwork literature exams test your recall and understanding of key characters, their roles, and their fates in the prescribed text (*Cry, the Beloved Country*).\n* **The Thought Process (How to Unpack It):**\n  1. *Step 1 — Read both columns fully:* Scan Column A to identify who each person is, and read Column B to understand the descriptions provided.\n  2. *Step 2 — Link character to plot/role:* Connect Arthur Jarvis with his activism (D), his father James Jarvis with his grief (C), Msimangu with his priesthood and kindness (E), and Absalom with his crime and trial (A).\n  3. *Step 3 — Adhere to instructions:* Check the command word/instruction. If it asks for *'only the letter'*, do not write out the full description, as failing to follow negative or restrictive constraints can lead to losing marks.\n* **How DBE Markers Score It (The Marking Formula):**\n  * 1 mark per correct match, provided the formatting instruction (writing only the letter) is followed.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always check the instruction for matching questions. If it says \"Write only the letter\", do not write the description out. Writing extra information can sometimes negate your answer if it contradicts your letter!"
